WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.697 --> 00:00:05.030
MANDY WAITE: Welcome, everybody.

00:00:05.030 --> 00:00:05.530
Hello.

00:00:05.530 --> 00:00:06.750
How's it all going?

00:00:06.750 --> 00:00:07.865
Good to see you all here.

00:00:07.865 --> 00:00:10.130
Thanks for coming to
this talk, specifically.

00:00:10.130 --> 00:00:12.650
There's many, many talks here
all going on at the same time.

00:00:12.650 --> 00:00:15.320
So I'm really, really
pleased to see you here, so

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:16.900
thank you for coming.

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:19.420
And I hope you really
get a good chance

00:00:19.420 --> 00:00:22.600
to enjoy all of Next,
particularly upstairs

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:24.670
on the third floor,
the showcase, which

00:00:24.670 --> 00:00:27.970
we spent the last three
or four months building.

00:00:27.970 --> 00:00:29.990
And we think it's a
lot of fun, so we'd

00:00:29.990 --> 00:00:31.180
love to see you up there.

00:00:31.180 --> 00:00:33.730
If you have any questions,
I'll be crawling around there,

00:00:33.730 --> 00:00:34.300
generally.

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:36.440
And if you kind of get
stuck and need help,

00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:39.160
if you see anybody wearing a
jacket like this with developer

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:42.010
relations on the back, you can
come up and ask us questions

00:00:42.010 --> 00:00:44.410
and we will make sure that
you get them answered for you.

00:00:44.410 --> 00:00:45.100
OK?

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:46.910
So welcome, thank
you for coming.

00:00:46.910 --> 00:00:51.010
We're going to talk about design
and secure user experience

00:00:51.010 --> 00:00:52.000
into your products.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:54.820
And my name is Mandy Waite,
I am a developer advocate.

00:00:54.820 --> 00:00:56.560
I work on Google Cloud--

00:00:56.560 --> 00:00:58.420
Google Cloud Platform
specifically-- now we

00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:00.980
have this more wider umbrella.

00:01:00.980 --> 00:01:05.436
And you can find me on
Twitter at @tekgrrl, OK?

00:01:05.436 --> 00:01:07.810
So please feel free to tweet
me if you have any questions

00:01:07.810 --> 00:01:09.820
that we don't answer today.

00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:13.860
So yeah, how many of
you are developers?

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:15.096
Wow.

00:01:15.096 --> 00:01:15.720
That's awesome.

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:18.360
How many of you
and not developers?

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:19.410
OK, interesting.

00:01:19.410 --> 00:01:21.160
That's the other half,
because some people

00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:23.437
didn't put their hands
up, which is interesting.

00:01:23.437 --> 00:01:25.820
OK.

00:01:25.820 --> 00:01:28.720
We're just going to have more
of a developer focus, generally.

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:32.520
But we are going to talk
about some UX stuff upfront

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:35.820
before we get to the
more detailed things

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:37.284
that developers will care about.

00:01:37.284 --> 00:01:38.700
The agenda is
something like this.

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:41.760
I don't normally do agendas,
so building good security

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:42.900
UX is where we start.

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:43.700
We're going to go through that.

00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:46.033
Then we're going to look at
something called Google Safe

00:01:46.033 --> 00:01:46.590
Browsing.

00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:50.380
How many of you heard of
Google Safe Browsing before?

00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:51.070
Excellent.

00:01:51.070 --> 00:01:53.350
Most of you have used it.

00:01:53.350 --> 00:01:55.340
Most of you have used
it without probably

00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:56.890
knowing that you were using it.

00:01:56.890 --> 00:01:59.350
We'll get into that shortly.

00:01:59.350 --> 00:02:01.330
Security UX.

00:02:01.330 --> 00:02:06.140
We are in a world where
security is vital.

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:10.180
We need to build security
into everything that we build.

00:02:10.180 --> 00:02:12.250
It should never really
be an afterthought,

00:02:12.250 --> 00:02:14.320
but it has for a long time been.

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:17.910
So we want to talk
about how this works

00:02:17.910 --> 00:02:19.892
within the context of Google.

00:02:19.892 --> 00:02:22.100
Not necessary in the context
of building new products

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:24.070
generally, but more to
all of the experience

00:02:24.070 --> 00:02:26.620
that we've gained
over the years.

00:02:26.620 --> 00:02:28.420
And also look at
the tools that we

00:02:28.420 --> 00:02:30.490
provide that can
actually help you build

00:02:30.490 --> 00:02:32.790
secure UX into your products.

00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:36.297
OK so this is a bridge.

00:02:36.297 --> 00:02:37.630
It's a good picture of a bridge.

00:02:37.630 --> 00:02:38.870
It's got guardrails.

00:02:38.870 --> 00:02:41.720
Gaurdrails protect
pedestrians from--

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:43.330
and probably cyclists as well--

00:02:43.330 --> 00:02:46.330
from the traffic on one side
and the water on the other.

00:02:46.330 --> 00:02:50.030
They do a very effective
job of that as well.

00:02:50.030 --> 00:02:53.500
And as do these things as
well-- these interstitial--

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:58.330
these warnings that pop up that
people see on a regular basis.

00:02:58.330 --> 00:03:03.250
These effectively protect people
from things that are ahead.

00:03:03.250 --> 00:03:08.030
Things like phishing, or
malware, or unwanted software

00:03:08.030 --> 00:03:09.195
generally.

00:03:09.195 --> 00:03:11.320
These things are really
powerful and really useful,

00:03:11.320 --> 00:03:16.180
but they are hard to design
and are very hard to get right.

00:03:16.180 --> 00:03:18.280
So I'd like to talk
today about some of that

00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:21.100
and how that works.

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:25.030
So warnings, ultimately, attempt
to convince users to stop

00:03:25.030 --> 00:03:26.300
doing what they're doing.

00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:26.800
OK?

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:28.470
You know how easy
that is, right?

00:03:28.470 --> 00:03:30.880
To convince people to stop
doing what they're doing.

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:32.060
No, I will do this.

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:34.120
I will do this.

00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:36.400
And it's a fine art.

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:37.600
It's a trade off.

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:38.690
It's checks and balances.

00:03:38.690 --> 00:03:40.600
There's a lot of things
involved in getting this right,

00:03:40.600 --> 00:03:42.295
and there's a lot of
research involved.

00:03:42.295 --> 00:03:46.170
I'm hoping that we can
show you some of that.

00:03:46.170 --> 00:03:48.520
So why would a user
ignore a warning

00:03:48.520 --> 00:03:51.430
like the one we just saw
in the previous page?

00:03:51.430 --> 00:03:53.020
Why we would ignore that?

00:03:53.020 --> 00:03:55.570
Or more importantly the bigger
one, which is like this.

00:03:55.570 --> 00:03:59.190
You see these, why
would you ignore it?

00:03:59.190 --> 00:04:02.440
And there are generally
three main reasons

00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:04.480
why people might ignore it--

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:05.480
there are more.

00:04:05.480 --> 00:04:07.990
These are the ones we
think are important.

00:04:07.990 --> 00:04:11.800
Today, in this talk, all of them
are important, and all of them

00:04:11.800 --> 00:04:13.760
should be given
into consideration.

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:16.220
But the ones we really
want to mention today

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:19.540
are the fact that some
interstitial like that

00:04:19.540 --> 00:04:23.380
will likely be interrupting
what a user is doing.

00:04:23.380 --> 00:04:25.090
Something they want
to actually get done,

00:04:25.090 --> 00:04:27.340
and it may well be that
they're under kind of pressure

00:04:27.340 --> 00:04:28.786
to get it done as well.

00:04:28.786 --> 00:04:29.910
So they want to move ahead.

00:04:29.910 --> 00:04:31.451
They want to go to
and visit the site

00:04:31.451 --> 00:04:34.082
that they visited regularly--
maybe not very long ago.

00:04:34.082 --> 00:04:36.040
They want to download
the software because they

00:04:36.040 --> 00:04:38.200
know that this software is
good, but these warnings

00:04:38.200 --> 00:04:42.040
are coming up
telling them to stop.

00:04:42.040 --> 00:04:45.040
That's one reason why
people might ignore it.

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:47.960
Also the situation may
be highly technical.

00:04:47.960 --> 00:04:51.940
And again this plays into
what we do-- as people,

00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:54.370
we develop products,
not necessarily

00:04:54.370 --> 00:04:56.074
the situation itself.

00:04:56.074 --> 00:04:58.490
Even highly technical situations
can be explained in a way

00:04:58.490 --> 00:05:00.880
that people can understand.

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:03.880
We'll go into that in
more detail very shortly.

00:05:03.880 --> 00:05:05.410
Sometimes the risk
just isn't clear.

00:05:05.410 --> 00:05:07.618
It may be something you do
on a regular basis, right?

00:05:07.618 --> 00:05:10.452
You go to this site
every day, and today it's

00:05:10.452 --> 00:05:11.410
broken for some reason.

00:05:11.410 --> 00:05:14.160
Today it's coming up
with this big red sign,

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:16.386
and you don't
think that's right.

00:05:16.386 --> 00:05:18.510
I don't know what the risk
is, I don't really care.

00:05:18.510 --> 00:05:20.617
I'm going to click on
this and go ahead anyway.

00:05:20.617 --> 00:05:23.200
So there's some of the reasons
that people may ignore warnings

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:25.557
like that.

00:05:25.557 --> 00:05:27.390
There are some additional
challenges as well

00:05:27.390 --> 00:05:28.200
that come up.

00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:30.950
We really-- by developing
these interstitial

00:05:30.950 --> 00:05:32.400
and you yourself
in the products--

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:35.160
you don't want to stop people
doing the right thing-- doing

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:37.830
something that they
should be entitled to do.

00:05:37.830 --> 00:05:40.890
But we also need to make
sure that we keep a very low

00:05:40.890 --> 00:05:42.150
false positive rate as well.

00:05:42.150 --> 00:05:45.070
We need to gain some
trust into the system.

00:05:45.070 --> 00:05:48.770
If we just fail all of the time
and are unreliable in that,

00:05:48.770 --> 00:05:54.240
or not a trusted advisor then
we lose people's mind share.

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:56.550
They're likely to have less
trust in us in the future

00:05:56.550 --> 00:05:59.718
when we display these
kind of messages.

00:05:59.718 --> 00:06:04.780
And ultimately, this slide is
here to show the dancing pig

00:06:04.780 --> 00:06:06.870
because the dancing pig
is funny, and it's cute.

00:06:06.870 --> 00:06:09.100
And at this time of
day you need something

00:06:09.100 --> 00:06:11.470
like that to uplift you
because it's that kind of day.

00:06:11.470 --> 00:06:12.511
We're just over the hump.

00:06:12.511 --> 00:06:15.040
We've had lunch, and
we need dancing pigs.

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:17.020
And ultimately,
it's reasonably true

00:06:17.020 --> 00:06:19.510
that given a choice between
dancing pigs and security,

00:06:19.510 --> 00:06:21.540
users will pick dancing pigs.

00:06:21.540 --> 00:06:27.000
Now again, that's not the
kind of undermines security

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:27.670
in any way.

00:06:27.670 --> 00:06:30.420
Dancing pigs are
interesting, but sometimes

00:06:30.420 --> 00:06:32.705
going to the dancing
pigs may be dangerous.

00:06:32.705 --> 00:06:34.830
So let's not worry too much
about the dancing pigs.

00:06:34.830 --> 00:06:37.550
I don't know if they are
dangerous, but they could be.

00:06:37.550 --> 00:06:40.350
All right, so that was a
quote from Edward Felten--

00:06:40.350 --> 00:06:45.530
professor of computer
science at Princeton.

00:06:45.530 --> 00:06:47.710
I just moved to America by
the way, four months ago.

00:06:47.710 --> 00:06:50.168
I've lived here for four months,
it's wonderful, I like it.

00:06:50.168 --> 00:06:53.370
I live-- in San Francisco,
which is where we are today--

00:06:53.370 --> 00:06:55.230
it's strange.

00:06:55.230 --> 00:06:56.070
Comprehension.

00:06:56.070 --> 00:06:57.870
So we talked about--

00:06:57.870 --> 00:07:01.590
we've set the scene
for secure UX.

00:07:01.590 --> 00:07:05.580
Some of the key components of
that-- the key considerations

00:07:05.580 --> 00:07:06.980
are around comprehension.

00:07:06.980 --> 00:07:08.730
So we're going to talk
about comprehension

00:07:08.730 --> 00:07:11.230
first before we move
on to the next stage.

00:07:11.230 --> 00:07:12.600
So what does comprehension mean?

00:07:12.600 --> 00:07:15.420
Comprehension ultimately
means, the users

00:07:15.420 --> 00:07:17.940
understand what is going on.

00:07:17.940 --> 00:07:21.120
They see these warnings,
maybe other information

00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:23.670
you provide as part of
your user interface that

00:07:23.670 --> 00:07:25.590
is based around security.

00:07:25.590 --> 00:07:27.240
And when people read
this information--

00:07:27.240 --> 00:07:29.190
when they see these
interstitial--

00:07:29.190 --> 00:07:31.140
that kind of thing,
pop-ups, they

00:07:31.140 --> 00:07:33.630
understand exactly
what's going on.

00:07:33.630 --> 00:07:35.040
This is key.

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:37.290
It is confusing, people won't
be able to understand it

00:07:37.290 --> 00:07:39.544
and won't be able to make
it do the right thing.

00:07:39.544 --> 00:07:40.960
We want them to
do the right thing

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:43.960
and we'll talk about
that shortly as well.

00:07:43.960 --> 00:07:44.985
So comprehension is--

00:07:47.565 --> 00:07:49.440
I'm kind of interested
in this first sentence

00:07:49.440 --> 00:07:51.330
because I originally
wrote it, and I kind of

00:07:51.330 --> 00:07:53.130
changed my mind on it.

00:07:53.130 --> 00:07:55.072
But I wanted to leave
it there anyway.

00:07:55.072 --> 00:07:57.280
The user should be able to
make an informed decision.

00:07:57.280 --> 00:07:59.760
Now, ultimately really
with these things,

00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:01.770
we don't want them
to make a decision.

00:08:01.770 --> 00:08:04.410
We want them to do the thing
that we want them to do.

00:08:04.410 --> 00:08:06.440
So this shouldn't
really be a decision,

00:08:06.440 --> 00:08:08.070
but we want to make
the right choice.

00:08:08.070 --> 00:08:10.470
We want them to
actually say, "They

00:08:10.470 --> 00:08:14.427
want me to skip going
ahead to the website.

00:08:14.427 --> 00:08:16.260
They don't want me to
download the software.

00:08:16.260 --> 00:08:17.530
I'm going to do it."

00:08:17.530 --> 00:08:19.170
But ultimately, it
does play into that

00:08:19.170 --> 00:08:21.210
because you have to kind
of balance the odds.

00:08:21.210 --> 00:08:23.680
You have to take all of
the information available

00:08:23.680 --> 00:08:25.390
and make a decision.

00:08:25.390 --> 00:08:27.720
Will I click on this,
"back to safety" button,

00:08:27.720 --> 00:08:31.038
or will I find a way to
circumvent it somehow by,

00:08:31.038 --> 00:08:32.579
maybe digging into
these menus, which

00:08:32.579 --> 00:08:34.704
may give me an option to
go forward which is really

00:08:34.704 --> 00:08:36.510
what I want to do.

00:08:36.510 --> 00:08:38.370
So it's kind of interesting.

00:08:38.370 --> 00:08:41.700
It's a real trade off and
a real balancing game.

00:08:41.700 --> 00:08:44.070
Ultimately, what
it comes down to is

00:08:44.070 --> 00:08:46.260
the user should be
able to understand

00:08:46.260 --> 00:08:47.855
the exact source of the threat.

00:08:47.855 --> 00:08:49.230
They should be
able to understand

00:08:49.230 --> 00:08:50.750
exactly what's at risk.

00:08:50.750 --> 00:08:52.770
They're about send their
password to somebody

00:08:52.770 --> 00:08:56.850
they don't trust because
of some phishing scam.

00:08:56.850 --> 00:08:59.780
They also need to understand the
likelihood of it being false--

00:08:59.780 --> 00:09:03.630
a false positive
warning, which may well

00:09:03.630 --> 00:09:05.280
undermine the whole system.

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:07.230
They're used to getting
false positives,

00:09:07.230 --> 00:09:09.286
and ultimately that may
mean that they have very

00:09:09.286 --> 00:09:10.410
little trust in the system.

00:09:13.780 --> 00:09:18.280
So Microsoft have--
well, some time ago--

00:09:18.280 --> 00:09:20.520
did a study and
they called it Neat.

00:09:20.520 --> 00:09:25.150
And Neat is-- based on
these principles here--

00:09:25.150 --> 00:09:28.615
we would like to simultaneously
accomplish these things--

00:09:28.615 --> 00:09:29.490
all of them together.

00:09:29.490 --> 00:09:30.864
And as you can
see some of them--

00:09:30.864 --> 00:09:33.190
there's a bit of a tension
between some of them.

00:09:33.190 --> 00:09:36.100
We want it to be presented
in a non-technical language.

00:09:36.100 --> 00:09:37.960
We can't make any
assumptions about people's

00:09:37.960 --> 00:09:39.150
technical background.

00:09:39.150 --> 00:09:43.690
You people here may
well be a fully versed

00:09:43.690 --> 00:09:46.199
with all of the kind of
technology that's out there.

00:09:46.199 --> 00:09:48.490
And we can present you with
stack traces and such like,

00:09:48.490 --> 00:09:51.145
and you'll say, ah, it's a
stack trace I like this one.

00:09:51.145 --> 00:09:53.270
It was a Java stack trace
and it's about that long,

00:09:53.270 --> 00:09:54.686
and I actually
can't find anything

00:09:54.686 --> 00:09:58.250
meaningful in it, which is
always quite interesting.

00:09:58.250 --> 00:10:01.180
We want to present it
in a non-technical way.

00:10:01.180 --> 00:10:04.000
Reading level should
be fairly low.

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:06.110
We want to make sure that
everybody can read it.

00:10:06.110 --> 00:10:08.800
And again, not everybody is
a native English speaker.

00:10:08.800 --> 00:10:12.400
Not everybody is old,
or older, like we are--

00:10:12.400 --> 00:10:14.500
or like I am at least.

00:10:14.500 --> 00:10:16.721
We want things to be as
brief as possible, as well.

00:10:16.721 --> 00:10:18.220
We don't want people
to have to read

00:10:18.220 --> 00:10:20.960
from reams and reams and
reams of verbiage just

00:10:20.960 --> 00:10:23.390
to find out what's going on.

00:10:23.390 --> 00:10:26.210
We want to be very specific
about the risk as well.

00:10:26.210 --> 00:10:27.960
And we want to provide
enough information.

00:10:27.960 --> 00:10:30.400
And as you can see,
there's some tension there.

00:10:30.400 --> 00:10:33.820
Be as brief as possible,
provide enough information.

00:10:33.820 --> 00:10:35.640
You go to get that balance.

00:10:35.640 --> 00:10:38.650
Number one would be being
very specific about the risk,

00:10:38.650 --> 00:10:42.280
but not using any kind
of technical jargon--

00:10:42.280 --> 00:10:44.354
that also makes it
more complicated.

00:10:44.354 --> 00:10:45.520
There is some tensions here.

00:10:45.520 --> 00:10:47.410
You do your best, and
you do some trade offs,

00:10:47.410 --> 00:10:51.364
and we'll talk yet about
some of that more shortly.

00:10:51.364 --> 00:10:52.280
How are you all doing?

00:10:52.280 --> 00:10:54.950
You all good?

00:10:54.950 --> 00:10:57.730
One question, was it warm
in the keynote today?

00:10:57.730 --> 00:10:59.100
Some people say-- oh, no?

00:10:59.100 --> 00:11:02.040
Oh, interesting.

00:11:02.040 --> 00:11:03.752
I was fighting fires
out in the showcase,

00:11:03.752 --> 00:11:05.960
so I didn't get to see it,
but I will watch it later.

00:11:09.390 --> 00:11:11.430
What we don't aim to do.

00:11:11.430 --> 00:11:14.070
We are not trying to
accomplish providing

00:11:14.070 --> 00:11:16.750
some kind of complete
description of the issue.

00:11:16.750 --> 00:11:18.800
That would probably
go back to what we

00:11:18.800 --> 00:11:20.280
looked on the previous slide.

00:11:20.280 --> 00:11:23.304
Some of the things we want
to accomplish simultaneously.

00:11:23.304 --> 00:11:25.470
One thing is we don't want
to do is actually provide

00:11:25.470 --> 00:11:28.230
a complete description
of the issue.

00:11:28.230 --> 00:11:31.200
Will not provide any information
for software engineers.

00:11:31.200 --> 00:11:33.000
Again, we can do.

00:11:33.000 --> 00:11:34.464
and often at Google,
we see things

00:11:34.464 --> 00:11:35.630
that other people don't see.

00:11:35.630 --> 00:11:38.170
Slight traces and such
like when things go wrong,

00:11:38.170 --> 00:11:40.832
which is quite interesting
and useful for us engineers.

00:11:40.832 --> 00:11:43.290
And sometimes it probably is
a bit frustrating for software

00:11:43.290 --> 00:11:45.940
engineers to see these kind of
interstitials and you'd say,

00:11:45.940 --> 00:11:49.440
hmm, I want to know more
about what happened.

00:11:49.440 --> 00:11:50.100
We can do that.

00:11:50.100 --> 00:11:50.891
We can handle that.

00:11:50.891 --> 00:11:54.246
We can provide-- learn more
links or advance drop downs

00:11:54.246 --> 00:11:56.370
that people can use to
actually find out more about

00:11:56.370 --> 00:11:57.360
what's going on--

00:11:57.360 --> 00:11:58.767
drill deeper into the problem.

00:11:58.767 --> 00:12:00.600
And there's also things
like developer tools

00:12:00.600 --> 00:12:02.225
and suchlike that
can actually help you

00:12:02.225 --> 00:12:03.907
understand better what went on.

00:12:03.907 --> 00:12:05.490
So you could be using
Chrome web tools

00:12:05.490 --> 00:12:09.187
and such like to find
exactly what happened

00:12:09.187 --> 00:12:10.020
underneath the hood.

00:12:12.781 --> 00:12:13.780
This is where we end up.

00:12:13.780 --> 00:12:16.210
We end up with these kind
of lovely red interstitial,

00:12:16.210 --> 00:12:20.050
and the bright color
is a very strong one.

00:12:20.050 --> 00:12:21.910
Maybe one day I
can ask Alison why

00:12:21.910 --> 00:12:24.970
we choose to have this kind
of very strong red color.

00:12:24.970 --> 00:12:27.160
But it does provide
that kind of warning.

00:12:27.160 --> 00:12:30.040
Do not go ahead,
this is dangerous.

00:12:30.040 --> 00:12:31.670
It's hard to ignore.

00:12:31.670 --> 00:12:33.670
So we're going to talk
more about these shortly.

00:12:33.670 --> 00:12:35.795
But these come from a
product called Safe Browsing,

00:12:35.795 --> 00:12:39.650
and we are going to talk in
detail about that very shortly.

00:12:39.650 --> 00:12:43.070
And the next thing is
beyond comprehension.

00:12:43.070 --> 00:12:46.289
We need to think
about adherence.

00:12:46.289 --> 00:12:48.830
This again is making sure people
do the right thing, which is

00:12:48.830 --> 00:12:51.580
the thing we want them to do.

00:12:51.580 --> 00:12:56.470
So adherence is--
users do what you want.

00:12:56.470 --> 00:12:57.974
That's important.

00:12:57.974 --> 00:12:59.890
We're not putting these
things up for a laugh.

00:12:59.890 --> 00:13:01.723
We're putting them up
because it is serious.

00:13:01.723 --> 00:13:04.300
We've detected some
kind of threat ahead.

00:13:04.300 --> 00:13:07.100
and we'll look at competence
levels and suchlike shortly,

00:13:07.100 --> 00:13:10.660
but we really wanting them to
do the thing we tell them to do.

00:13:10.660 --> 00:13:13.990
So we can measure that.

00:13:13.990 --> 00:13:16.390
Let's talk about
exactly how we can

00:13:16.390 --> 00:13:18.310
make it more likely
that people will

00:13:18.310 --> 00:13:20.110
do the thing that you want.

00:13:20.110 --> 00:13:23.920
So opinionated design is the
first tenant around that.

00:13:23.920 --> 00:13:25.840
We need the design
to be reasonably

00:13:25.840 --> 00:13:30.222
opinionated about what we
want you to do as a user.

00:13:30.222 --> 00:13:32.680
So we've had great success when
the user interface provides

00:13:32.680 --> 00:13:35.170
things like, very
clear instructions.

00:13:35.170 --> 00:13:38.720
It provides an attractive
preferred choice.

00:13:38.720 --> 00:13:42.480
Again, the way we present
it may play into this.

00:13:42.480 --> 00:13:45.520
It provides an
unattractive other choice,

00:13:45.520 --> 00:13:49.060
or it provides no
other choice at all.

00:13:49.060 --> 00:13:51.160
Being very strongly
opinionated we may say,

00:13:51.160 --> 00:13:53.410
this file is infected
with a virus.

00:13:53.410 --> 00:13:56.140
We are not going to
let you download it.

00:13:56.140 --> 00:13:59.650
And that's an extremely
opinionated view of the world.

00:13:59.650 --> 00:14:01.390
And often you don't
go that far, but you

00:14:01.390 --> 00:14:03.730
may be prepared to
if you are very, very

00:14:03.730 --> 00:14:06.310
confident that the
threat is there and real.

00:14:06.310 --> 00:14:08.920
The very act of downloading an
infected file into your system

00:14:08.920 --> 00:14:11.530
can automatically
infect your system.

00:14:11.530 --> 00:14:13.300
Let's be thorough about that.

00:14:13.300 --> 00:14:19.412
So looking at this one,
this is the current download

00:14:19.412 --> 00:14:21.870
interstitial aught to pop-pop
the way you've seen nowadays.

00:14:21.870 --> 00:14:24.490
If you try to download a file
that's infected with something

00:14:24.490 --> 00:14:26.620
and we've detected
that it's infected,

00:14:26.620 --> 00:14:27.970
we will tell you to discard it.

00:14:27.970 --> 00:14:31.250
We said the file is dangerous,
so Chrome has blocked it.

00:14:31.250 --> 00:14:35.140
And when you click on the down
arrow you find out, learn more.

00:14:35.140 --> 00:14:38.050
You don't say, eh, ignore
my warning and go ahead.

00:14:38.050 --> 00:14:41.140
You say, learn more and we
can tell you more about it,

00:14:41.140 --> 00:14:44.720
but we really do not
want you to download it.

00:14:44.720 --> 00:14:46.460
You think about why
we're doing this.

00:14:46.460 --> 00:14:49.240
We want to reduce
infection rates.

00:14:49.240 --> 00:14:53.260
We want to reduce infected
sites across the globe.

00:14:53.260 --> 00:14:57.080
We want to make the internet
a safer place for everybody.

00:14:57.080 --> 00:15:00.650
And this is where tools such
as Safe Browsing playing in.

00:15:00.650 --> 00:15:01.830
So here's an example.

00:15:01.830 --> 00:15:04.760
And I'm going to dive into
some older content note

00:15:04.760 --> 00:15:06.190
that's been around for a while.

00:15:06.190 --> 00:15:08.530
So some of these screens
is going to be fairly old,

00:15:08.530 --> 00:15:10.270
but I think it's
still interesting

00:15:10.270 --> 00:15:12.730
because they show some of
the ways we've progressed

00:15:12.730 --> 00:15:17.170
over the way we see these kind
of security user experience.

00:15:17.170 --> 00:15:19.570
And you probably all at
some point in your life--

00:15:19.570 --> 00:15:22.360
unless you're extremely
young-- seen this one, which is

00:15:22.360 --> 00:15:25.840
the HTTPS warning from Chrome.

00:15:25.840 --> 00:15:30.430
So Chrome 2014-ish would
of display this certificate

00:15:30.430 --> 00:15:32.890
if this certificate--
this message--

00:15:32.890 --> 00:15:35.310
if SSL wasn't working correctly.

00:15:35.310 --> 00:15:38.830
If it seemed that you were
trying to go to a site

00:15:38.830 --> 00:15:41.920
that didn't have a
valid certificate.

00:15:41.920 --> 00:15:45.010
And we're not very
opinionated here.

00:15:45.010 --> 00:15:48.430
We say, proceed
anyway, back to safety.

00:15:48.430 --> 00:15:51.780
So who thinks this
is opinionated?

00:15:51.780 --> 00:15:53.690
Who thinks this is
not opinionated?

00:15:53.690 --> 00:15:56.430
OK, right.

00:15:56.430 --> 00:15:58.330
It's not really opinionated.

00:15:58.330 --> 00:15:59.810
We offer two choices.

00:15:59.810 --> 00:16:02.620
The first one is,
proceed anyway.

00:16:02.620 --> 00:16:04.330
The other one is back to safety.

00:16:04.330 --> 00:16:06.476
Now there is some background
around HTTPS warnings

00:16:06.476 --> 00:16:09.100
that you should probably know--
we're going to get too shortly.

00:16:09.100 --> 00:16:11.710
But really we're not
being very opinionated.

00:16:11.710 --> 00:16:15.379
We should really want
them to go back to safety.

00:16:15.379 --> 00:16:17.170
They may have more
information about what's

00:16:17.170 --> 00:16:20.589
going on than we
do, but we do really

00:16:20.589 --> 00:16:21.880
want them to go back to safety.

00:16:21.880 --> 00:16:24.426
But we've offered them this
option to proceed anyway.

00:16:24.426 --> 00:16:26.050
We have a help me
understand link which

00:16:26.050 --> 00:16:28.837
may actually clarify the issue.

00:16:28.837 --> 00:16:30.420
What you are looking
at today, at what

00:16:30.420 --> 00:16:32.770
we had today within Chrome.

00:16:32.770 --> 00:16:35.030
And this is the
HTTPS warning today.

00:16:35.030 --> 00:16:36.550
Your connection is not private.

00:16:36.550 --> 00:16:39.010
Attackers might be trying to
steal your information from,

00:16:39.010 --> 00:16:39.531
whatever.

00:16:39.531 --> 00:16:42.030
They may be trying to get your
passwords, and your messages,

00:16:42.030 --> 00:16:42.946
and your credit cards.

00:16:42.946 --> 00:16:44.470
This is important stuff.

00:16:44.470 --> 00:16:46.680
You don't want to give
this stuff away to people.

00:16:46.680 --> 00:16:49.570
I'm your kind of person who
actually tears labels off

00:16:49.570 --> 00:16:55.030
of boxes and envelopes and
shreds them or compresses them

00:16:55.030 --> 00:16:56.840
and throws them
away-- such a way

00:16:56.840 --> 00:16:58.590
that no body can ever
read them, so nobody

00:16:58.590 --> 00:17:00.950
can get any kind of
information from me.

00:17:00.950 --> 00:17:02.770
You do not want to
share your password,

00:17:02.770 --> 00:17:05.710
and your social security
number in the clear.

00:17:05.710 --> 00:17:07.810
So we'd really want
you to not do this.

00:17:07.810 --> 00:17:10.119
You may have additional
information, as I say,

00:17:10.119 --> 00:17:12.640
that may lead you to want
to go ahead and do it.

00:17:12.640 --> 00:17:14.951
So we have a big blue
"back to safety" button.

00:17:14.951 --> 00:17:17.200
So now, we don't want it to
be a red button, obviously

00:17:17.200 --> 00:17:19.060
because red is not
going to be help here.

00:17:19.060 --> 00:17:21.140
So a nice blue one, a
nice color that people

00:17:21.140 --> 00:17:22.680
can actually respond to.

00:17:22.680 --> 00:17:24.849
If the need to find out
more they can go to advance.

00:17:24.849 --> 00:17:27.700
And if they need to, they
have other information

00:17:27.700 --> 00:17:32.370
that we're not aware of
they can choose to proceed.

00:17:32.370 --> 00:17:36.400
So that's opinionated,
very opinionated.

00:17:36.400 --> 00:17:37.980
So a couple more
examples, and these

00:17:37.980 --> 00:17:41.490
are older interstitial,
or pop-ups

00:17:41.490 --> 00:17:43.500
that we've had in the past.

00:17:43.500 --> 00:17:46.440
The first one is
somewhat more or less

00:17:46.440 --> 00:17:48.120
opinionated than the first one.

00:17:48.120 --> 00:17:51.450
Both are a little bit
borderline opinionated.

00:17:51.450 --> 00:17:53.730
If you can see the first
one, important point

00:17:53.730 --> 00:17:56.100
here-- adherence
can be measured.

00:17:56.100 --> 00:17:56.940
You have control.

00:17:56.940 --> 00:18:00.624
You can understand what users
do when they see these pop-ups.

00:18:00.624 --> 00:18:01.290
What do they do?

00:18:01.290 --> 00:18:02.320
What step did they take?

00:18:02.320 --> 00:18:04.950
Did they choose
to keep the file?

00:18:04.950 --> 00:18:06.600
Did they choose to
discard the file?

00:18:06.600 --> 00:18:09.570
We can measure that, and
that's what's called adherence.

00:18:09.570 --> 00:18:13.060
How many people decided to
actually heed your warning.

00:18:13.060 --> 00:18:15.510
So in this case with the
first one, 72% of people

00:18:15.510 --> 00:18:17.810
decided, yeah, OK, fair cop.

00:18:17.810 --> 00:18:21.780
I'm British, by the way,
so I say colloquialisms

00:18:21.780 --> 00:18:24.320
like fair cop, mate.

00:18:24.320 --> 00:18:31.410
72% people actually decided
to discard in the first case,

00:18:31.410 --> 00:18:33.060
which isn't very high.

00:18:33.060 --> 00:18:35.340
It's not bad, but it's
not where we want to be.

00:18:35.340 --> 00:18:37.630
We want to be at a
100% if possible.

00:18:37.630 --> 00:18:39.420
The bottom one is better.

00:18:39.420 --> 00:18:40.926
We're saying the
file is malicious.

00:18:40.926 --> 00:18:42.800
We have a strong opinion
that it's malicious.

00:18:42.800 --> 00:18:44.570
We're not saying it
appears malicious.

00:18:44.570 --> 00:18:46.860
We're saying, hey, this
thing is malicious.

00:18:46.860 --> 00:18:47.940
Don't download it.

00:18:47.940 --> 00:18:50.440
But we still offer
a couple of options.

00:18:50.440 --> 00:18:52.090
And 93% adherence rate here.

00:18:52.090 --> 00:18:53.340
So people did the right thing.

00:18:53.340 --> 00:18:55.330
People did the thing that
we wanted them to do,

00:18:55.330 --> 00:18:57.970
and much more frequently than
they did with the first case.

00:19:01.150 --> 00:19:03.290
Oh, sorry about that.

00:19:03.290 --> 00:19:05.770
Something's changed.

00:19:05.770 --> 00:19:08.470
I'm sure somebody is editing
my slides when I'm talking

00:19:08.470 --> 00:19:11.820
or I've just made last
minute changes that

00:19:11.820 --> 00:19:12.760
didn't come right--

00:19:12.760 --> 00:19:13.760
OK.

00:19:13.760 --> 00:19:14.262
Good.

00:19:14.262 --> 00:19:15.345
We will ignore this slide.

00:19:15.345 --> 00:19:18.260
This slide is a mistake,
but we will get back to it

00:19:18.260 --> 00:19:19.111
very shortly.

00:19:23.320 --> 00:19:24.360
Warning fatigue.

00:19:24.360 --> 00:19:26.180
As you can imagine--

00:19:26.180 --> 00:19:29.720
and you've probably
experienced this yourself--

00:19:29.720 --> 00:19:33.110
if you ever used Windows
7 back in the early days

00:19:33.110 --> 00:19:35.990
when it first launched and
you had to approve everything.

00:19:35.990 --> 00:19:38.210
You had so many pop-ups
it was unbelievable,

00:19:38.210 --> 00:19:40.050
or maybe that was
Windows 8, I'm not sure.

00:19:40.050 --> 00:19:43.730
But warning fatigue--
how many times have you

00:19:43.730 --> 00:19:46.900
had warning fatigue where
the warning keeps popping up.

00:19:46.900 --> 00:19:48.497
It seems like the same one.

00:19:48.497 --> 00:19:50.330
You're not quite sure
if it is the same one,

00:19:50.330 --> 00:19:52.360
but it does seem to be
annoying and irritating,

00:19:52.360 --> 00:19:55.240
and doesn't seem to be
particularly relevant.

00:19:55.240 --> 00:19:57.560
There is evidence
of warning fatigue,

00:19:57.560 --> 00:19:58.760
but you have to be careful.

00:19:58.760 --> 00:20:00.468
And we have to think
about this and we'll

00:20:00.468 --> 00:20:03.090
try to get to the
bottom of that shortly.

00:20:03.090 --> 00:20:05.180
So this is again,
HTTPS warnings.

00:20:05.180 --> 00:20:08.012
We're looking at adherents
from different countries,

00:20:08.012 --> 00:20:09.470
and I'll leave that
to you to guess

00:20:09.470 --> 00:20:12.560
which countries they are because
I don't know all of them.

00:20:12.560 --> 00:20:14.330
I do not know what
TR is, for example,

00:20:14.330 --> 00:20:16.760
and I should have checked.

00:20:16.760 --> 00:20:18.810
Number of prior
warning impressions.

00:20:18.810 --> 00:20:23.360
So basically, the first time
you see this warning pop up,

00:20:23.360 --> 00:20:27.920
around about 75% to 77%
of people on average

00:20:27.920 --> 00:20:31.110
would do the thing that
you wanted them to do.

00:20:31.110 --> 00:20:33.530
But after a while-- after
five of these within a period

00:20:33.530 --> 00:20:34.310
of time--

00:20:34.310 --> 00:20:37.390
people get kind of fatigued
by the whole process,

00:20:37.390 --> 00:20:38.900
and that they're
much less likely,

00:20:38.900 --> 00:20:42.170
or significantly less
likely-- in this case around

00:20:42.170 --> 00:20:44.660
get's dropped down around 50%--

00:20:44.660 --> 00:20:47.540
are likely to follow
your instructions,

00:20:47.540 --> 00:20:54.300
or follow your advice, and
that's not a great situation.

00:20:54.300 --> 00:20:56.100
But this isn't just
about warning fatigue.

00:20:56.100 --> 00:21:00.030
It's also about many,
many different vectors,

00:21:00.030 --> 00:21:02.010
particularly around what
type of threat it is

00:21:02.010 --> 00:21:03.720
and how you present it.

00:21:03.720 --> 00:21:05.380
So all these things
need consideration,

00:21:05.380 --> 00:21:09.270
which is why we're going to talk
about measurement very shortly.

00:21:09.270 --> 00:21:14.330
And just to kind of contrast,
unwanted software warnings.

00:21:14.330 --> 00:21:15.080
This is different.

00:21:15.080 --> 00:21:16.560
This is different behavior.

00:21:16.560 --> 00:21:19.920
So, UWS, unwanted software.

00:21:19.920 --> 00:21:21.660
So this is the
same kind of thing.

00:21:21.660 --> 00:21:24.570
Adherence is high to begin with.

00:21:24.570 --> 00:21:26.640
People generally don't
want unwanted software.

00:21:26.640 --> 00:21:28.920
These things are just annoying.

00:21:28.920 --> 00:21:29.970
You didn't ask for it.

00:21:29.970 --> 00:21:32.640
It's not like it's blocking you
from doing something you really

00:21:32.640 --> 00:21:33.630
want to do.

00:21:33.630 --> 00:21:36.462
It's just like a pop-up
saying, download this.

00:21:36.462 --> 00:21:37.920
OK, I'm not going
to download that.

00:21:37.920 --> 00:21:39.090
I don't know what it is.

00:21:39.090 --> 00:21:42.900
You're probably going to say,
ignore, get rid of, delete,

00:21:42.900 --> 00:21:44.819
whatever.

00:21:44.819 --> 00:21:45.735
But at the same time--

00:21:48.960 --> 00:21:51.579
the first time you're going
to see it is around 95%.

00:21:51.579 --> 00:21:53.120
But as you get more
and more of them,

00:21:53.120 --> 00:21:56.190
you're even more likely, not
significantly, but even more

00:21:56.190 --> 00:22:00.090
likely to follow the
instructions to discard it,

00:22:00.090 --> 00:22:01.230
so that's significant.

00:22:01.230 --> 00:22:02.730
We need to understand
the difference

00:22:02.730 --> 00:22:06.690
between showing HTTPS warnings
and showing unwanted software

00:22:06.690 --> 00:22:07.847
warnings.

00:22:07.847 --> 00:22:09.430
So this all comes
back to measurement,

00:22:09.430 --> 00:22:12.900
which we'll talk about shortly.

00:22:12.900 --> 00:22:17.090
So false alarms, and
repeats warnings as well.

00:22:17.090 --> 00:22:19.072
This is quite
interesting, slow kittens.

00:22:19.072 --> 00:22:20.780
I'm sorry, I pulled
this one out earlier.

00:22:20.780 --> 00:22:23.090
We have to mention
kittens in Google Talk,

00:22:23.090 --> 00:22:25.800
it's a rule of the company.

00:22:25.800 --> 00:22:28.040
So each false alarm
reduces the credibility

00:22:28.040 --> 00:22:29.120
of a warning system.

00:22:29.120 --> 00:22:36.470
This is a fairly old quote from
a white paper, slow kittens.

00:22:36.470 --> 00:22:39.790
And just to talk
about that, we found

00:22:39.790 --> 00:22:42.102
that people don't respond
favorably to repeat warnings.

00:22:42.102 --> 00:22:44.310
And now we're talking about
repeat of the same thing.

00:22:44.310 --> 00:22:46.610
It's clearly the same thing
being repeated over and over

00:22:46.610 --> 00:22:47.110
again.

00:22:49.430 --> 00:22:52.867
If you do something, if you
click or make a decision

00:22:52.867 --> 00:22:54.950
to do something based on
one of these interstitial

00:22:54.950 --> 00:22:57.980
we should remember it
for some period of time.

00:22:57.980 --> 00:23:00.260
But what's the ideal
period of time?

00:23:00.260 --> 00:23:02.310
Should it be a day,
should it be a week,

00:23:02.310 --> 00:23:05.240
or actually be an hour before
we display that message again?

00:23:05.240 --> 00:23:09.825
If you get an SSL warning and
decide to continue shouldn't we

00:23:09.825 --> 00:23:12.170
just remember that forever?

00:23:12.170 --> 00:23:14.000
We'll always let you
go there, or should we

00:23:14.000 --> 00:23:15.386
constantly remind you?

00:23:15.386 --> 00:23:17.510
Should be constantly remind
you every five minutes?

00:23:17.510 --> 00:23:22.040
Because we remind you
every day or every week?

00:23:22.040 --> 00:23:24.890
And there's this whole
thing about regrets as well.

00:23:24.890 --> 00:23:27.260
Some people regret
that decision.

00:23:27.260 --> 00:23:29.960
They decide to move
forward to go to a site

00:23:29.960 --> 00:23:33.110
that they don't trust, or to
download some malware that they

00:23:33.110 --> 00:23:36.510
shouldn't download, and maybe
they regret that decision.

00:23:36.510 --> 00:23:39.130
And you have these things
called the adherence rate, which

00:23:39.130 --> 00:23:41.720
we already talked about and
also the regret rate as well.

00:23:41.720 --> 00:23:44.794
That's harder to measure and
I don't know a huge amount

00:23:44.794 --> 00:23:47.210
about it, [INAUDIBLE] started
reading this in white paper.

00:23:47.210 --> 00:23:49.190
But if you are
interested in that,

00:23:49.190 --> 00:23:50.990
come and talk to me afterwards.

00:23:50.990 --> 00:23:55.070
But there is a sweet spot
here over about a week--

00:23:55.070 --> 00:23:59.720
if we remember your decision
as a user for about a week,

00:23:59.720 --> 00:24:02.220
and then represent that
again a week later then that

00:24:02.220 --> 00:24:04.464
seems to be about
the right sweet spot

00:24:04.464 --> 00:24:06.130
in terms of getting
the adherence right.

00:24:06.130 --> 00:24:07.630
And in terms of making
sure that people

00:24:07.630 --> 00:24:08.796
don't regret their decision.

00:24:12.100 --> 00:24:13.450
So let's talk about measurement.

00:24:13.450 --> 00:24:15.824
Well, I kind of mentioned
measurement quite a lot so far.

00:24:18.930 --> 00:24:21.440
And I'm not going to
go into huge details,

00:24:21.440 --> 00:24:24.240
I want to get into Safe
Browsing and talk about that.

00:24:24.240 --> 00:24:26.850
How many of you came here
to talk about Safe Browsing

00:24:26.850 --> 00:24:28.268
specifically?

00:24:28.268 --> 00:24:29.040
OK, no.

00:24:29.040 --> 00:24:29.540
Good.

00:24:29.540 --> 00:24:31.581
So we're going to hopefully
introduce you to that

00:24:31.581 --> 00:24:33.180
and enlighten you.

00:24:33.180 --> 00:24:34.070
Measurement.

00:24:34.070 --> 00:24:35.920
Measurement is important.

00:24:35.920 --> 00:24:37.150
And here's some measurements.

00:24:37.150 --> 00:24:39.729
And again, these go
back awhile but they're

00:24:39.729 --> 00:24:41.770
from a really good document--
a good presentation

00:24:41.770 --> 00:24:44.760
that's been presented
before back in 2014.

00:24:44.760 --> 00:24:47.910
Chrome's malware interstitial
use to look like this.

00:24:47.910 --> 00:24:51.230
And it's again,
strongly opinionated.

00:24:51.230 --> 00:24:54.330
Here it has a go back option,
has an advanced option.

00:24:54.330 --> 00:24:56.800
Our confidence in this-- to
the fact that there is malware

00:24:56.800 --> 00:24:59.320
behind whatever
you're trying to do--

00:24:59.320 --> 00:25:02.020
is extremely strong.

00:25:02.020 --> 00:25:07.000
And upwards of-- my
clock just reset--

00:25:07.000 --> 00:25:09.580
the confidence is
extremely strong.

00:25:09.580 --> 00:25:12.580
So we're really confident
that what you're trying to do

00:25:12.580 --> 00:25:14.450
is download malware.

00:25:14.450 --> 00:25:18.650
We're telling you that
it's malware, and people--

00:25:18.650 --> 00:25:21.310
25% of them nearly--
are still choosing

00:25:21.310 --> 00:25:23.980
to proceed and actually
download malware.

00:25:23.980 --> 00:25:25.540
That's a little bit scary.

00:25:25.540 --> 00:25:26.660
We need to do better.

00:25:26.660 --> 00:25:31.250
We should always be aiming
to get 100% adherence.

00:25:31.250 --> 00:25:35.110
This is effectively
77% adherence.

00:25:35.110 --> 00:25:36.540
If we look at these--

00:25:36.540 --> 00:25:38.080
and again comparing
to-- we're going

00:25:38.080 --> 00:25:41.200
to compare Chrome and Firefox.

00:25:41.200 --> 00:25:44.260
We see the old nonfatal
SSL warning in Chrome,

00:25:44.260 --> 00:25:47.380
proceed anyway or
back to safety.

00:25:47.380 --> 00:25:48.980
We have less
confidence about this.

00:25:48.980 --> 00:25:50.770
I think it's a little
bit more than that.

00:25:50.770 --> 00:25:52.603
I think the graphic
people who have actually

00:25:52.603 --> 00:25:55.250
adjusted it a little bit,
but it's not that high.

00:25:55.250 --> 00:25:57.750
It's reasonable but
it's not that high.

00:25:57.750 --> 00:26:00.290
There are many
different reasons why

00:26:00.290 --> 00:26:03.180
an SSL certificate
may be invalid,

00:26:03.180 --> 00:26:05.380
and it may not be a threat.

00:26:05.380 --> 00:26:07.180
It may be the SSL
[INAUDIBLE] certificate

00:26:07.180 --> 00:26:09.090
for example, which means
it doesn't tie back

00:26:09.090 --> 00:26:10.750
into a route certificate.

00:26:10.750 --> 00:26:15.580
So there are many reasons why
there may be problems with SSL

00:26:15.580 --> 00:26:18.520
that we would flag as being
something problematic.

00:26:18.520 --> 00:26:20.410
So we have less confidence here.

00:26:20.410 --> 00:26:24.370
And we found the click through
rate will be around 68%.

00:26:24.370 --> 00:26:28.870
68% of people decided to
proceed to the site anyway.

00:26:28.870 --> 00:26:30.705
Now, what is it?

00:26:30.705 --> 00:26:32.830
Is it the nature of the
warning that's the problem,

00:26:32.830 --> 00:26:34.852
or is it the way it's presented?

00:26:34.852 --> 00:26:36.310
Well, let's take
a look at Firefox.

00:26:36.310 --> 00:26:39.860
At Firefox, at exactly the same
time had this interstitial.

00:26:39.860 --> 00:26:42.894
This connection is untrusted,
get me out of here.

00:26:42.894 --> 00:26:45.310
We've all seen this as well
if you've used Firefox before.

00:26:45.310 --> 00:26:46.184
"Get me out of here."

00:26:46.184 --> 00:26:48.217
It's fairly clear,
is very opinionated.

00:26:48.217 --> 00:26:50.050
We have the same level
of confidence though.

00:26:50.050 --> 00:26:52.060
This is still SSL,
there's nothing different

00:26:52.060 --> 00:26:54.467
when you're using
Firefox and Chrome,

00:26:54.467 --> 00:26:56.050
but you can see the
click through rate

00:26:56.050 --> 00:26:58.560
is now down to 33%.

00:26:58.560 --> 00:26:59.680
So this is important.

00:26:59.680 --> 00:27:02.650
This is an important
measurement.

00:27:02.650 --> 00:27:05.160
We have to improve on that.

00:27:05.160 --> 00:27:08.260
If you see a 68% click
through, and you're pretty sure

00:27:08.260 --> 00:27:11.000
you don't want them to
continue you need to fix that.

00:27:11.000 --> 00:27:13.450
But only by measuring it
can you actually understand

00:27:13.450 --> 00:27:15.659
how to fix it, what
to do, what you

00:27:15.659 --> 00:27:17.200
need to change in
the way you message

00:27:17.200 --> 00:27:19.379
this, the way you
present it to the user.

00:27:19.379 --> 00:27:21.420
The only way you can fix
that is by measuring it.

00:27:24.910 --> 00:27:26.830
Measurement is
ultimately the key

00:27:26.830 --> 00:27:31.150
to how effective comprehension
and adherence are.

00:27:31.150 --> 00:27:33.280
You do your AB
testing, you maybe

00:27:33.280 --> 00:27:36.040
have different versions
the interstitial,

00:27:36.040 --> 00:27:38.600
and we've done that
many, many times.

00:27:38.600 --> 00:27:41.380
Alison, are we doing that
kind of AB testing currently

00:27:41.380 --> 00:27:43.430
with Safe Browsing?

00:27:43.430 --> 00:27:43.930
Yep.

00:27:43.930 --> 00:27:46.610
So we do this kind of AB
testing all of the time.

00:27:46.610 --> 00:27:48.610
And we can get the
statistics on what people do,

00:27:48.610 --> 00:27:50.110
and we can work out
and fine tune it

00:27:50.110 --> 00:27:52.880
until we get it exactly right.

00:27:52.880 --> 00:27:55.250
Click through rates,
adherence, effectively

00:27:55.250 --> 00:27:57.760
can vary based on a number
of different factors

00:27:57.760 --> 00:28:01.240
as we saw from the
Firefox, Chrome comparison.

00:28:01.240 --> 00:28:03.020
And there's always
room for improvement.

00:28:03.020 --> 00:28:04.750
So do more and more
measurement is always

00:28:04.750 --> 00:28:07.210
going to get you better results.

00:28:07.210 --> 00:28:10.180
And you may often
see bad things,

00:28:10.180 --> 00:28:12.910
worse things happen when you go
a certain path, which case you

00:28:12.910 --> 00:28:14.480
can course correct.

00:28:14.480 --> 00:28:16.850
But you should never be doing
these things in isolation.

00:28:16.850 --> 00:28:19.800
Roll them out two at
a time, do AB testing.

00:28:22.409 --> 00:28:23.450
So that's the first part.

00:28:23.450 --> 00:28:29.660
So we talked about
secure UX generally.

00:28:29.660 --> 00:28:32.900
Let's talk about Google and
the whole context of Google.

00:28:32.900 --> 00:28:36.860
We have a lot of experience
observing traffic.

00:28:36.860 --> 00:28:38.990
We do it a lot, as
you can imagine.

00:28:38.990 --> 00:28:40.810
And we do it in a lot
of different ways,

00:28:40.810 --> 00:28:42.812
a lot of different
types of traffic.

00:28:42.812 --> 00:28:44.270
So we have a lot
of experience when

00:28:44.270 --> 00:28:48.847
it comes to actually building
secure UX into our products.

00:28:48.847 --> 00:28:50.180
And again, that's still ongoing.

00:28:50.180 --> 00:28:52.138
It's always going to be
an ongoing effort based

00:28:52.138 --> 00:28:54.710
on the feedback that we get
from people's interactions

00:28:54.710 --> 00:28:56.050
with our products.

00:28:56.050 --> 00:28:59.600
And what we want to do is
also talk about something

00:28:59.600 --> 00:29:03.350
that can actually aid you
in building more secure user

00:29:03.350 --> 00:29:06.624
experience into your products
by using this particular product

00:29:06.624 --> 00:29:07.790
called Google Safe Browsing.

00:29:11.245 --> 00:29:14.710
This is a bit of a play
on Google's vision, which

00:29:14.710 --> 00:29:20.060
is ultimately-- make the world's
information safely accessible.

00:29:20.060 --> 00:29:21.680
Really doing a lot
of work upfront

00:29:21.680 --> 00:29:23.480
to make it easier to
access information.

00:29:23.480 --> 00:29:27.030
Not only easier, sorry,
but also safer as well.

00:29:27.030 --> 00:29:29.090
So making it easier has
been part of the mission.

00:29:29.090 --> 00:29:31.880
Making it safely accessible
is the other part of it.

00:29:31.880 --> 00:29:34.610
And we're going to talk
about this in more detail.

00:29:34.610 --> 00:29:37.460
Again, these lovely red signs--

00:29:37.460 --> 00:29:40.430
interstitial-- these
come from Safe Browsing.

00:29:40.430 --> 00:29:43.694
So as I say, you all
probably used Safe Browsing

00:29:43.694 --> 00:29:45.110
before because
you've all probably

00:29:45.110 --> 00:29:47.120
seen warnings like this before.

00:29:47.120 --> 00:29:49.070
You've clicked on
the link, maybe

00:29:49.070 --> 00:29:51.530
it was through some phishing
email-- hopefully not.

00:29:51.530 --> 00:29:53.780
Or maybe it was just a link
that turned up some where.

00:29:53.780 --> 00:29:55.410
You clicked on it,
hopefully these things

00:29:55.410 --> 00:29:56.743
don't turn up in search results.

00:29:56.743 --> 00:29:59.510
We can get rid of those,
but at some point in time

00:29:59.510 --> 00:30:01.850
you will see these
kind of interstitial.

00:30:01.850 --> 00:30:05.020
The left one is the
Chrome interstitial,

00:30:05.020 --> 00:30:06.740
the other one is
Chrome and Android.

00:30:12.160 --> 00:30:13.760
Oh, interesting.

00:30:13.760 --> 00:30:15.020
My speaker notes are here.

00:30:15.020 --> 00:30:15.940
It's amazing.

00:30:15.940 --> 00:30:17.760
[LAUGHS] I didn't
expect to see that.

00:30:20.870 --> 00:30:23.090
So how does Google
Safe Browsing work?

00:30:23.090 --> 00:30:24.891
So basically we do crawl--

00:30:24.891 --> 00:30:26.390
as you know, as you
can imagine-- we

00:30:26.390 --> 00:30:28.220
crawl many, many pages.

00:30:28.220 --> 00:30:30.470
Lots and lots of
pages every day.

00:30:30.470 --> 00:30:33.380
Tens of millions sites
effectively looking

00:30:33.380 --> 00:30:35.510
for harmful content.

00:30:35.510 --> 00:30:38.810
And we also have
the notion of, we

00:30:38.810 --> 00:30:40.830
can get leads from
other sources as well--

00:30:40.830 --> 00:30:42.620
how they can also
play into this.

00:30:42.620 --> 00:30:44.835
And what the result is,
we will build blacklist

00:30:44.835 --> 00:30:46.580
from reputation
databases that can

00:30:46.580 --> 00:30:51.440
be used as the back end for
this safe browsing API that

00:30:51.440 --> 00:30:54.800
is built into, or built
into Chromes functionality

00:30:54.800 --> 00:30:56.505
and also into Firefox--

00:30:56.505 --> 00:30:58.010
and into other products as well.

00:31:01.420 --> 00:31:04.210
Safe Browsing, surprisingly
or maybe unsurprisingly--

00:31:04.210 --> 00:31:06.460
if you've seen it a few
times-- is it effectively

00:31:06.460 --> 00:31:09.290
protecting one billion
people globally.

00:31:09.290 --> 00:31:11.860
And while about 1.1 was the
last measurement we had.

00:31:11.860 --> 00:31:13.180
It's probably higher now.

00:31:13.180 --> 00:31:16.450
And ultimately
every day we crawl

00:31:16.450 --> 00:31:20.620
over 10 million URLS,
Chrome extensions,

00:31:20.620 --> 00:31:24.340
executable, android
applications every day.

00:31:24.340 --> 00:31:26.620
So we have good information
about what's infected

00:31:26.620 --> 00:31:27.400
and what's not.

00:31:27.400 --> 00:31:28.960
We have various
algorithms that can

00:31:28.960 --> 00:31:34.370
be used to detect that
kind of scams going forward

00:31:34.370 --> 00:31:39.560
and also infected software.

00:31:39.560 --> 00:31:44.120
We've detected around
about 50,000 harmful sites

00:31:44.120 --> 00:31:46.790
every week.

00:31:46.790 --> 00:31:49.280
And Safe Browsing
for Google is built

00:31:49.280 --> 00:31:51.290
into most of our products.

00:31:51.290 --> 00:31:55.030
So if we look at Google Play,
if you install an application,

00:31:55.030 --> 00:31:58.184
or upload an applications
into the Google Play Store,

00:31:58.184 --> 00:31:59.600
Safe Browsing will
come into play.

00:31:59.600 --> 00:32:01.516
It will be looking for
malicious content being

00:32:01.516 --> 00:32:02.810
uploaded to the Play Store.

00:32:02.810 --> 00:32:05.270
And also through Verify
Apps, it will also

00:32:05.270 --> 00:32:08.630
be used to protect when
you install from sources

00:32:08.630 --> 00:32:10.611
other than Google Play as well.

00:32:10.611 --> 00:32:12.860
It's built into Google Search,
so if there is infected

00:32:12.860 --> 00:32:17.450
malware out there, even if we do
return the search page for it--

00:32:17.450 --> 00:32:20.750
if we don't know it's infected
we would display that.

00:32:20.750 --> 00:32:23.310
Most of the time we will
filter that out there.

00:32:23.310 --> 00:32:24.206
Google Ads.

00:32:24.206 --> 00:32:25.580
Google Ads is
extremely important

00:32:25.580 --> 00:32:27.580
because when you see an
ad, and you click on it,

00:32:27.580 --> 00:32:29.000
it takes you to a link.

00:32:29.000 --> 00:32:30.700
We don't want to take
you to a page that

00:32:30.700 --> 00:32:35.190
may be a phishing scam or
providing malicious content.

00:32:35.190 --> 00:32:37.400
So this is built
into Ads as well.

00:32:37.400 --> 00:32:39.360
It's built into Gmail,
in terms of Gmail,

00:32:39.360 --> 00:32:41.540
it protects users by
identifying things

00:32:41.540 --> 00:32:44.120
as links in email messages.

00:32:44.120 --> 00:32:47.000
And it shows warnings
if you just on them.

00:32:47.000 --> 00:32:51.500
Also it protects the Chrome Web
Store from potentially harmful

00:32:51.500 --> 00:32:52.640
extensions.

00:32:52.640 --> 00:32:57.480
So this is Google Safe Browsing
built into our products.

00:32:57.480 --> 00:32:59.240
But we also have
Safe Browsing support

00:32:59.240 --> 00:33:02.160
for many of the browsers
that are out there as well.

00:33:02.160 --> 00:33:04.610
So it protects things
like Google Chrome,

00:33:04.610 --> 00:33:08.660
it protects Firefox, Safari,
and also Chrome on Android.

00:33:11.820 --> 00:33:13.890
Ultimately-- I kind of
ruined the next slide--

00:33:13.890 --> 00:33:16.800
it protects around about
two billion devices.

00:33:16.800 --> 00:33:20.380
So that's a really
ridiculous high number.

00:33:20.380 --> 00:33:22.610
Two billion devices are
protected by Safe Browsing

00:33:22.610 --> 00:33:28.870
through things like Chrome,
Firefox, and Chrome on Android.

00:33:28.870 --> 00:33:31.620
So we also show around
about 50 million warnings

00:33:31.620 --> 00:33:33.390
to users on a weekly basis.

00:33:33.390 --> 00:33:35.120
So those red
interstitial you see

00:33:35.120 --> 00:33:40.170
appear around about over
50 million times a week.

00:33:40.170 --> 00:33:43.765
We also provide support
for Webmasters and also

00:33:43.765 --> 00:33:44.980
for developers.

00:33:44.980 --> 00:33:51.030
So we build in Safe Browsing
into Safety Net within Android,

00:33:51.030 --> 00:33:54.280
and that lets mobile developers
check URLS against the Safe

00:33:54.280 --> 00:33:55.540
Browsing lists.

00:33:55.540 --> 00:33:58.370
It's in fact the
actual same API that's

00:33:58.370 --> 00:34:01.300
used by Chrome on Android.

00:34:01.300 --> 00:34:04.360
And thousands of developers
and network administrators

00:34:04.360 --> 00:34:06.700
use the Safe Browsing
API to protect

00:34:06.700 --> 00:34:10.110
their platform and their users.

00:34:10.110 --> 00:34:12.940
If you can imagine that tens
and thousands of sites get

00:34:12.940 --> 00:34:16.850
hacked every day to serve
harmful content ultimately.

00:34:16.850 --> 00:34:19.030
Safe Browsing
ultimately notifies

00:34:19.030 --> 00:34:22.940
Webmasters of site owners that
their sites have been infected.

00:34:22.940 --> 00:34:24.790
So we have this
notification process,

00:34:24.790 --> 00:34:25.874
which you can sign up for.

00:34:25.874 --> 00:34:27.706
We have a link at the
end that will show you

00:34:27.706 --> 00:34:28.750
how to get to that.

00:34:28.750 --> 00:34:30.270
But basically, if
you're a Webmaster

00:34:30.270 --> 00:34:33.010
and you're signed up for
Google Webmaster's, you

00:34:33.010 --> 00:34:35.170
can get access to all
of these notifications.

00:34:35.170 --> 00:34:36.880
And they will tell
you if your sites

00:34:36.880 --> 00:34:39.090
are hosting malicious content.

00:34:42.449 --> 00:34:45.750
But ultimately, we have great
power, but with that reach

00:34:45.750 --> 00:34:48.159
comes great responsibility.

00:34:48.159 --> 00:34:51.150
And our challenge is to provide
the best protection for you

00:34:51.150 --> 00:34:53.990
while being very precise
with our results.

00:34:56.610 --> 00:34:58.620
So as you can imagine,
Google Safe Browsing

00:34:58.620 --> 00:35:00.780
is likely to be a target.

00:35:00.780 --> 00:35:04.420
The people who are creating
these malicious content--

00:35:04.420 --> 00:35:06.090
these malicious
sites-- are going

00:35:06.090 --> 00:35:11.370
to want to try to find a way
around our detection systems

00:35:11.370 --> 00:35:12.061
effectively.

00:35:12.061 --> 00:35:13.560
And there are various
different ways

00:35:13.560 --> 00:35:16.639
of doing that, which
we look at now.

00:35:16.639 --> 00:35:17.180
Bare with me.

00:35:25.020 --> 00:35:27.910
So here we see-- oh, the
lines are not very clear,

00:35:27.910 --> 00:35:29.070
at least on my screen--

00:35:29.070 --> 00:35:31.230
so here we have something
on the internet,

00:35:31.230 --> 00:35:32.740
some content being served.

00:35:32.740 --> 00:35:35.190
Maybe this is a site, maybe
it's some piece of content

00:35:35.190 --> 00:35:36.480
that's being served.

00:35:36.480 --> 00:35:39.170
And when Google Safe
Browsing comes to look at it,

00:35:39.170 --> 00:35:42.750
it sees that's OK,
there's no problems there.

00:35:42.750 --> 00:35:45.090
But when-- say we call
her Jane over there--

00:35:45.090 --> 00:35:46.724
she goes to download
the same content.

00:35:46.724 --> 00:35:48.390
She sees something
completely different.

00:35:48.390 --> 00:35:50.699
She sees malicious software.

00:35:50.699 --> 00:35:52.740
This could be a problem,
and there's various ways

00:35:52.740 --> 00:35:55.500
that attackers can
actually hide themselves

00:35:55.500 --> 00:35:57.270
from Google's detection.

00:35:57.270 --> 00:36:00.270
One of the most
common is IP cloaking.

00:36:00.270 --> 00:36:03.120
Effectively, serving
benign content

00:36:03.120 --> 00:36:06.870
to our detection systems, but
doing something very different

00:36:06.870 --> 00:36:08.910
when somebody else
on the internet

00:36:08.910 --> 00:36:12.270
comes to actually
access that content.

00:36:12.270 --> 00:36:14.670
And cyber criminals generally
don't spend too much time

00:36:14.670 --> 00:36:16.530
on the individual
vulnerabilities.

00:36:16.530 --> 00:36:18.790
They tend to move on to
different exploits very,

00:36:18.790 --> 00:36:20.080
very quickly.

00:36:20.080 --> 00:36:22.420
So you have to really
be ahead of the game.

00:36:22.420 --> 00:36:25.530
There's also thing called
Malvertising, malware

00:36:25.530 --> 00:36:28.760
being delivered through ads.

00:36:28.760 --> 00:36:30.780
It's a whole notion
of cloaking, it

00:36:30.780 --> 00:36:33.000
extends beyond just
the IP addresses.

00:36:33.000 --> 00:36:35.580
And our best protection,
where possible,

00:36:35.580 --> 00:36:39.420
is to appear exactly
like the end user traffic

00:36:39.420 --> 00:36:40.680
wherever we can.

00:36:40.680 --> 00:36:43.320
If we look like end
user traffic then

00:36:43.320 --> 00:36:45.810
our detection systems
will not be flagged,

00:36:45.810 --> 00:36:47.700
as being a detection system.

00:36:47.700 --> 00:36:50.470
We will see the content
that ordinary users will.

00:36:50.470 --> 00:36:52.020
So that's something
that's constantly

00:36:52.020 --> 00:36:53.680
ongoing within Safe Browsing.

00:36:56.229 --> 00:36:57.395
One story from the trenches.

00:36:57.395 --> 00:36:59.700
So I had to originally
[INAUDIBLE] a little bit more

00:36:59.700 --> 00:37:03.660
complicated to explain, so I'm
going to stick with this one.

00:37:03.660 --> 00:37:05.910
So you imagine you're
downloading software.

00:37:05.910 --> 00:37:07.560
You're using the
browser, potentially

00:37:07.560 --> 00:37:10.670
to download software.

00:37:10.670 --> 00:37:12.720
The download is complete.

00:37:12.720 --> 00:37:15.180
We have to decide whether
we should flag the content

00:37:15.180 --> 00:37:17.940
as malicious or not.

00:37:17.940 --> 00:37:20.880
The browser will do
certain things upfront.

00:37:20.880 --> 00:37:23.650
It can have some level of trust.

00:37:23.650 --> 00:37:25.290
It's a safe file type.

00:37:25.290 --> 00:37:27.570
This safe file type
is one that's not

00:37:27.570 --> 00:37:29.340
likely to infect the system.

00:37:29.340 --> 00:37:32.580
And so we can say, that's
fine, we will download that.

00:37:32.580 --> 00:37:35.542
We will provide it to the user,
they will be able to access it.

00:37:35.542 --> 00:37:37.500
If it's a potentially
harmful file, [INAUDIBLE]

00:37:37.500 --> 00:37:39.750
then we need to go to
the next level which is,

00:37:39.750 --> 00:37:41.786
does it come from a
trusted source or not?

00:37:41.786 --> 00:37:43.910
And if it comes from a
trusted source, that's fine.

00:37:43.910 --> 00:37:44.510
Great.

00:37:44.510 --> 00:37:46.680
OK, we know that
source, we can trust it,

00:37:46.680 --> 00:37:49.070
we can download the file
and provide it to the user.

00:37:49.070 --> 00:37:50.564
It it's an untrusted
source, we're

00:37:50.564 --> 00:37:52.230
still not completely
confident with them

00:37:52.230 --> 00:37:54.120
there's going to
be a problem here.

00:37:54.120 --> 00:37:56.280
At that point we want to
pull in Safe Browsing--

00:37:56.280 --> 00:37:57.840
send the content
to Safe Browsing,

00:37:57.840 --> 00:38:00.360
and have it decide for us
as to whether the content is

00:38:00.360 --> 00:38:01.004
malicious.

00:38:01.004 --> 00:38:03.420
At that point we can make a
decision on whether it's safe,

00:38:03.420 --> 00:38:05.610
or whether it's harmful.

00:38:05.610 --> 00:38:07.080
In this case, a
trusted source is

00:38:07.080 --> 00:38:11.370
important to note to be either
local, somewhere on your file

00:38:11.370 --> 00:38:14.570
system, or it could be a
whiteless domain, or a trusted

00:38:14.570 --> 00:38:15.600
sign up.

00:38:15.600 --> 00:38:19.452
So that's important here because
what you could potentially do--

00:38:19.452 --> 00:38:22.320
got my clicker-- what you
could potentially do here

00:38:22.320 --> 00:38:24.740
is break that chain.

00:38:24.740 --> 00:38:27.340
If you can get the
download completed,

00:38:27.340 --> 00:38:29.550
but then you can invoke
some JavaScript code,

00:38:29.550 --> 00:38:34.050
you can actually make the
file appear on the file system

00:38:34.050 --> 00:38:38.760
and then sort it back to
the user via JavaScript.

00:38:38.760 --> 00:38:42.210
So the browser actually believes
that the file was sourced

00:38:42.210 --> 00:38:44.460
from a local file system--

00:38:44.460 --> 00:38:46.380
one of these trusted
sources-- and that way

00:38:46.380 --> 00:38:49.110
can now bypass the system
because it's a trusted

00:38:49.110 --> 00:38:50.850
source and it would end here.

00:38:50.850 --> 00:38:52.810
So that's a real problem.

00:38:52.810 --> 00:38:55.110
And that's the kind
of detection issues

00:38:55.110 --> 00:38:56.790
that we have to deal with.

00:38:56.790 --> 00:39:00.900
And harmful JavaScript
can cause potential issues

00:39:00.900 --> 00:39:03.881
when it comes to bypassing
Safe Browsing security.

00:39:06.770 --> 00:39:10.160
So let's talk about
partnerships as well.

00:39:10.160 --> 00:39:12.610
Safe Browsing data is
very hard and takes

00:39:12.610 --> 00:39:14.690
some tremendous
amounts of resources,

00:39:14.690 --> 00:39:17.091
most of the challenges
are scale and evasion.

00:39:17.091 --> 00:39:19.340
But we want to make sure our
work can have the biggest

00:39:19.340 --> 00:39:21.216
impact as possible.

00:39:21.216 --> 00:39:24.260
For that we need to
work with other people,

00:39:24.260 --> 00:39:27.800
with third parties-- with
you people-- on integration.

00:39:27.800 --> 00:39:29.220
So we can't do it alone.

00:39:29.220 --> 00:39:30.720
And I mentioned
earlier that we have

00:39:30.720 --> 00:39:33.170
sources that can provide
more input into the list

00:39:33.170 --> 00:39:34.550
that we build.

00:39:34.550 --> 00:39:37.310
So we collaborate with
partners both inside

00:39:37.310 --> 00:39:39.090
and outside of Google.

00:39:39.090 --> 00:39:42.500
And we also partner
with external companies

00:39:42.500 --> 00:39:46.190
to combat harmful content
and protect users.

00:39:46.190 --> 00:39:48.140
There are free
main tools that you

00:39:48.140 --> 00:39:50.760
can use to protect your users.

00:39:50.760 --> 00:39:53.630
So the first one is we protect
network and content owners.

00:39:53.630 --> 00:39:58.320
So we can-- by using
the Safe Browsing

00:39:58.320 --> 00:40:00.380
for network and
content owners program,

00:40:00.380 --> 00:40:02.570
you can actually
have us send you

00:40:02.570 --> 00:40:05.150
notifications when we
detect harmful content

00:40:05.150 --> 00:40:08.420
on your network-- if it's
hosted on your network.

00:40:08.420 --> 00:40:10.021
And currently we
have around about--

00:40:10.021 --> 00:40:11.770
and this is a little
bit of an old number,

00:40:11.770 --> 00:40:13.340
it may be higher now--

00:40:13.340 --> 00:40:18.350
roughly around about 22,000
ASNs autonomous system numbers

00:40:18.350 --> 00:40:19.310
subscribed.

00:40:19.310 --> 00:40:21.830
That's around about
40% of active ASNs

00:40:21.830 --> 00:40:23.570
out there currently.

00:40:23.570 --> 00:40:26.000
So the ASNs effectively
uniquely identifies

00:40:26.000 --> 00:40:28.390
each network on the internet.

00:40:28.390 --> 00:40:29.930
So we can help there.

00:40:29.930 --> 00:40:32.180
We can help you understand--
on your own networks--

00:40:32.180 --> 00:40:34.940
whether you have any kind of
malicious content being hosted.

00:40:34.940 --> 00:40:37.380
And therefore help you to
avoid propagating it out

00:40:37.380 --> 00:40:39.110
to the rest of the
world, or internally

00:40:39.110 --> 00:40:41.510
within your own company.

00:40:41.510 --> 00:40:43.701
Also when it comes to
Webmasters-- again,

00:40:43.701 --> 00:40:45.950
we've mentioned this already--
with Google Webmaster's

00:40:45.950 --> 00:40:49.400
you can sign up by the Security
Council for notifications.

00:40:49.400 --> 00:40:53.072
We can check your sites
for malicious content.

00:40:53.072 --> 00:40:54.030
It's very, very simple.

00:40:54.030 --> 00:40:55.670
Very easy to get started with.

00:40:55.670 --> 00:40:58.520
You just had to verify
that you own the property,

00:40:58.520 --> 00:41:01.611
and then we can provide
information like this.

00:41:01.611 --> 00:41:03.110
And we're doing
this because we want

00:41:03.110 --> 00:41:04.910
to minimize the
impact on all users

00:41:04.910 --> 00:41:06.270
out there on the internet.

00:41:06.270 --> 00:41:07.100
So we can help you.

00:41:07.100 --> 00:41:10.260
Actually I understand that there
are issues on your own size.

00:41:10.260 --> 00:41:12.200
This is actually extremely
powerful as well.

00:41:15.130 --> 00:41:19.750
So that sends out around about
750,000 notifications per year.

00:41:19.750 --> 00:41:22.100
And it reduces the
duration of size

00:41:22.100 --> 00:41:25.460
being infected by around about
60%, which is really important.

00:41:25.460 --> 00:41:27.970
So this kind of reduces
the need for Safe Browsing

00:41:27.970 --> 00:41:29.890
to actually display
interstitial like we've

00:41:29.890 --> 00:41:33.260
seen before because we're
reducing the infection rates.

00:41:33.260 --> 00:41:35.980
And also 80%
successful clean up.

00:41:35.980 --> 00:41:37.990
So these sites are
infected, many of them

00:41:37.990 --> 00:41:39.640
get cleaned up
very, very quickly.

00:41:39.640 --> 00:41:41.890
Without those notifications
they would not

00:41:41.890 --> 00:41:44.320
have been aware that they
were infected until maybe they

00:41:44.320 --> 00:41:47.170
had some other kind of report
that they were infected,

00:41:47.170 --> 00:41:51.310
so Webmaster's really
helps with that.

00:41:51.310 --> 00:41:53.730
We also-- for
developers-- we have APIs,

00:41:53.730 --> 00:41:56.050
and APIs that you can
build into your own code.

00:41:56.050 --> 00:41:58.383
And so this where we're going
to get a little bit deeper

00:41:58.383 --> 00:42:01.155
into the technical
aspects of this.

00:42:01.155 --> 00:42:03.490
So the first one
is the Lookup API.

00:42:03.490 --> 00:42:10.220
The Lookup API is used basically
to detect malicious content,

00:42:10.220 --> 00:42:15.150
detect content stored
URLs, and give you

00:42:15.150 --> 00:42:17.890
a report back as to what
the threat was seeing.

00:42:17.890 --> 00:42:22.100
It's very simple to use, but
there are some downsides.

00:42:22.100 --> 00:42:24.600
One downside is we
learn about your URLs.

00:42:24.600 --> 00:42:28.190
You might not want to
share those URLs with us.

00:42:28.190 --> 00:42:31.610
Limited Lookup volume, you
can't do that many Lookups

00:42:31.610 --> 00:42:34.070
but it's kind of
limited around that.

00:42:34.070 --> 00:42:36.020
And it's also a little
bit higher latency.

00:42:36.020 --> 00:42:39.980
So around about 150
milliseconds latency on average.

00:42:39.980 --> 00:42:43.400
That would play into
user experience too.

00:42:43.400 --> 00:42:45.857
If you had to check
every URL, you

00:42:45.857 --> 00:42:48.410
would have 150
milliseconds latency

00:42:48.410 --> 00:42:52.280
like we're in the
Safe Browsing API.

00:42:52.280 --> 00:42:54.170
There's also the Android
Lookup API as well.

00:42:54.170 --> 00:42:56.550
And again, this is ideal
for mobile applications.

00:42:56.550 --> 00:42:59.440
We have a link for this at
the end, how to get started.

00:42:59.440 --> 00:43:01.857
It provides significant
bandwidth savings, in the fact

00:43:01.857 --> 00:43:04.190
that you don't have to actually
go all the way to Google

00:43:04.190 --> 00:43:05.510
to do it.

00:43:05.510 --> 00:43:07.160
It preserves privacy
because you do not

00:43:07.160 --> 00:43:10.770
need to send URLs to
Google to be able to make

00:43:10.770 --> 00:43:12.830
the detections successful.

00:43:12.830 --> 00:43:15.320
And it's used by
Android Chrome today.

00:43:15.320 --> 00:43:17.900
The other thing is a combination
of the Lookup API, which

00:43:17.900 --> 00:43:20.150
we talked about earlier--
and there's something called

00:43:20.150 --> 00:43:21.840
the Update API.

00:43:21.840 --> 00:43:23.460
This scale is extremely
well and we're

00:43:23.460 --> 00:43:25.550
going to show you a
demo just shortly.

00:43:25.550 --> 00:43:26.750
It preserves privacy.

00:43:26.750 --> 00:43:29.750
Again, we do not need
to send URLs to Google.

00:43:29.750 --> 00:43:35.120
It's used by the major browsers
including Firefox, and Chrome.

00:43:35.120 --> 00:43:37.820
It does require client code,
but mostly developers so that's

00:43:37.820 --> 00:43:41.060
great, because you
like writing code.

