00:53:41.375 --> 00:53:43.540
so port 8080 on local host.

00:53:43.540 --> 00:53:45.930
And so we run this now.

00:53:45.930 --> 00:53:48.950
It will look at the database.

00:53:48.950 --> 00:53:51.395
Initially it looks at the
database and said it's stale

00:53:51.395 --> 00:53:53.150
and now it's
healthy What's going

00:53:53.150 --> 00:53:54.650
on here underneath the hood?

00:53:54.650 --> 00:53:56.289
We specify the database.

00:53:56.289 --> 00:53:57.330
We didn't do that before.

00:53:57.330 --> 00:53:58.345
We had to look up.

00:53:58.345 --> 00:53:59.720
If actually what
we're doing here

00:53:59.720 --> 00:54:02.150
is pulling down
those lists, those

00:54:02.150 --> 00:54:05.155
blacklists we had
before, reputation lists,

00:54:05.155 --> 00:54:07.710
and pulling them down locally
and caching them locally.

00:54:07.710 --> 00:54:09.543
So now what we can do
is we can make look up

00:54:09.543 --> 00:54:11.181
calls against a local database.

00:54:11.181 --> 00:54:13.430
Now the thing is about this
is that we are not storing

00:54:13.430 --> 00:54:15.420
the URLs in the database.

00:54:15.420 --> 00:54:17.870
We are storing
hashes of the URLs.

00:54:17.870 --> 00:54:19.370
So effectively,
what we do is we go

00:54:19.370 --> 00:54:23.510
for a process of
canonicalizing the URLs,

00:54:23.510 --> 00:54:25.250
then hashing URLs
and then taking

00:54:25.250 --> 00:54:26.630
the first four
bytes of that hash

00:54:26.630 --> 00:54:28.430
and using that as a lookup.

00:54:28.430 --> 00:54:30.299
So when we do lookups
locally, we're

00:54:30.299 --> 00:54:32.090
actually doing them in
much different ways.

00:54:32.090 --> 00:54:35.210
What we would do, we would
send in URLs to the API.

00:54:35.210 --> 00:54:39.060
Now when we send a URL to
the API itself directly,

00:54:39.060 --> 00:54:41.450
it's still doing the
same thing for us.

00:54:41.450 --> 00:54:45.470
The databases are stored on
the Safe Browsing all hashes.

00:54:45.470 --> 00:54:47.340
And what we do is when
we receive the URL is

00:54:47.340 --> 00:54:48.590
we can convert it into a hash.

00:54:48.590 --> 00:54:51.730
And then we compare that
to the stored lists.

00:54:51.730 --> 00:54:55.220
And effectively, what we do
for the lookup, for the update

00:54:55.220 --> 00:54:56.930
API is pull that
list down locally,

00:54:56.930 --> 00:55:00.110
so we can make calls
against it locally.

00:55:00.110 --> 00:55:02.780
We never have to send
those URLs to Google.

00:55:02.780 --> 00:55:05.450
We never have to make
additional calls to Google

00:55:05.450 --> 00:55:07.260
to get the results back.

00:55:07.260 --> 00:55:10.015
We have lower latency as well.

00:55:10.015 --> 00:55:12.140
I'm not going to go into
that since I'm running out

00:55:12.140 --> 00:55:14.390
of time, so I'm going to
