We saw most of these.

00:55:26.570 --> 00:55:30.740
Threat types, malware, social
engineering, unwanted software,

00:55:30.740 --> 00:55:33.190
unspecified threat types.

00:55:33.190 --> 00:55:35.810
Others as well, different
platforms that we support,

00:55:35.810 --> 00:55:40.550
also hash URL and digest
are threat entries.

00:55:40.550 --> 00:55:42.170
Now URL was obvious,
we saw that.

00:55:42.170 --> 00:55:44.850
We sent a URL to the
Safe Browsing API.

00:55:44.850 --> 00:55:46.630
But we could also
specify a hash as well.

00:55:46.630 --> 00:55:48.880
If we know what the hash is,
we can send that directly

00:55:48.880 --> 00:55:52.030
to the API, or to the
update version of the API.

00:55:52.030 --> 00:55:54.420
And we can do the same
for digest as well.

00:55:54.420 --> 00:55:59.276
So we can actually bypass any
URL to anything effectively.

00:55:59.276 --> 00:56:00.650
So ultimately,
Safe Browsing list

00:56:00.650 --> 00:56:06.070
contain hashes effectively,
or at least hash prefixes.

00:56:06.070 --> 00:56:08.120
The first four
bytes of the hash.

00:56:08.120 --> 00:56:10.825
This dramatically
reduces bandwidth,

00:56:10.825 --> 00:56:12.680
and makes life easier for us.

00:56:12.680 --> 00:56:15.740
This is a combination of the
update and the lookup API.

00:56:15.740 --> 00:56:20.540
Once we have SB server that
we saw earlier running,

00:56:20.540 --> 00:56:23.270
when we make calls against that,
we are effectively making calls

00:56:23.270 --> 00:56:26.920
to a version of a local version
of the lookup API, which is why

00:56:26.920 --> 00:56:30.600
it says update plus lookup API.

00:56:30.600 --> 00:56:32.460
So not it looks like
something like this.

00:56:32.460 --> 00:56:35.660
We have the hash prefix
database stored locally.

00:56:35.660 --> 00:56:37.820
The database
version is sent back

00:56:37.820 --> 00:56:39.980
to the Safe Browsing
and any additions,

00:56:39.980 --> 00:56:43.370
or removals are sent
back to the Safe Browsing

00:56:43.370 --> 00:56:44.682
local version of the database.

00:56:44.682 --> 00:56:46.640
And you saw that when we
went through the demo.

00:56:46.640 --> 00:56:48.620
And you saw that the
database was stale,

00:56:48.620 --> 00:56:50.121
and then the
database was healthy.

00:56:50.121 --> 00:56:51.620
And then it was
pulling down updates

00:56:51.620 --> 00:56:53.620
to make sure it had the
latest versions of them.

00:56:55.929 --> 00:56:57.470
So this is the whole
process by which

00:56:57.470 --> 00:56:59.570
those hashes are arrived at.

00:56:59.570 --> 00:57:02.210
We have this
canonicalization process,

00:57:02.210 --> 00:57:03.535
which you see on the left here.

00:57:03.535 --> 00:57:04.910
And also with that
it's converted

00:57:04.910 --> 00:57:07.640
to these four byte hashes which
is stored in the reputation

00:57:07.640 --> 00:57:08.870
databases.

00:57:08.870 --> 00:57:10.340
That's local.

00:57:10.340 --> 00:57:11.810
Whenever we want
to do a lookup, we

00:57:11.810 --> 00:57:15.450
will send back the prefix
back to Safe Browsing

00:57:15.450 --> 00:57:17.450
and we will get back a
list of results to match.

00:57:21.080 --> 00:57:24.260
And here's an example of
actual code written in Go.

